Task 1: Build the Ansible Project and Authenticate to the Cluster
Subtask 1.1: Create the Project Structure
mkdir -p ~/odf-troubleshooting-lab/{playbooks,inventory,templates}
cd ~/odf-troubleshooting-lab
Subtask 1.2: Configure Inventory and Connection Variables
Create the inventory file. This lab authenticates using the kubeconfig already active on the control node (from oc login), which is simpler and more reliable than passing raw tokens through Ansible variables.

cat > inventory/hosts.yml << 'EOF'
all:
  hosts:
    localhost:
      ansible_connection: local
      ansible_python_interpreter: "{{ ansible_playbook_python }}"
  vars:
    odf_namespace: "openshift-storage"
EOF
Verify that your current shell session is already authenticated to the cluster, since the playbooks in this lab rely on the active kubeconfig context rather than re-authenticating in Ansible:

oc whoami
oc get project openshift-storage
Deliverable: Running oc whoami returns your username, and oc get project openshift-storage returns the project without an error. This confirms the kubeconfig used by kubernetes.core modules is valid.

Subtask 1.3: Create a Basic Storage Health Check Playbook
This playbook uses kubernetes.core.k8s_info, which reads its connection details (server, token, certificate) from the active kubeconfig by default when no host/api_key parameters are given. This avoids duplicating authentication logic in every task.

cat > playbooks/storage-health-check.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: ODF Storage Health Check
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    odf_namespace: "openshift-storage"

  tasks:
    - name: Check ODF namespace status
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: Namespace
        name: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: odf_namespace_status

    - name: Display namespace status
      debug:
        msg: "ODF Namespace Status: {{ odf_namespace_status.resources[0].status.phase }}"
      when: odf_namespace_status.resources | length > 0

    - name: Get all storage classes
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: storage.k8s.io/v1
        kind: StorageClass
      register: storage_classes

    - name: Report ODF-related storage classes
      debug:
        msg: "Found storage class: {{ item.metadata.name }} (provisioner: {{ item.provisioner }})"
      loop: "{{ storage_classes.resources }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.metadata.name }}"
      when: "'ceph' in item.provisioner or 'ocs' in item.metadata.name"

    - name: Get Ceph cluster status
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: ceph.rook.io/v1
        kind: CephCluster
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: ceph_clusters

    - name: Display Ceph cluster health
      debug:
        msg: "Ceph Cluster {{ item.metadata.name }} phase: {{ item.status.phase | default('Unknown') }}"
      loop: "{{ ceph_clusters.resources }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.metadata.name }}"
      when: ceph_clusters.resources | length > 0
EOF
Run the playbook:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/storage-health-check.yml
Deliverable: The playbook completes with failed=0. The debug output shows the ODF namespace phase as Active, lists at least one storage class containing ceph in its provisioner (for example openshift-storage.rbd.csi.ceph.com), and reports a CephCluster phase (typically Ready on a healthy cluster). If the CephCluster task returns zero resources, this is expected on a minimal or non-ODF cluster and should be noted in your results rather than treated as a failure.

Task 2: Detect Common Storage Issues and Generate a Report
Subtask 2.1: Create the Issue-Detection Playbook
This playbook checks for two common ODF problems: PVCs stuck in Pending and Ceph OSD pods not in the Running phase.

cat > playbooks/storage-troubleshooting.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Detect Common ODF Storage Issues
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    odf_namespace: "openshift-storage"

  tasks:
    - name: Get all PVCs across the cluster
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
      register: all_pvcs

    - name: Identify pending PVCs
      set_fact:
        failed_pvcs: "{{ all_pvcs.resources | selectattr('status.phase', 'equalto', 'Pending') | list }}"

    - name: Report pending PVCs
      debug:
        msg: "Pending PVC: {{ item.metadata.name }} in namespace {{ item.metadata.namespace }}"
      loop: "{{ failed_pvcs }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.metadata.name }}"
      when: failed_pvcs | length > 0

    - name: Report when no pending PVCs are found
      debug:
        msg: "No pending PVCs detected."
      when: failed_pvcs | length == 0

    - name: Get Ceph OSD pods
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: Pod
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
        label_selectors:
          - "app=rook-ceph-osd"
      register: ceph_osd_pods

    - name: Identify unhealthy OSD pods
      set_fact:
        unhealthy_osds: "{{ ceph_osd_pods.resources | rejectattr('status.phase', 'equalto', 'Running') | list }}"

    - name: Report unhealthy OSD pods
      debug:
        msg: "Unhealthy OSD Pod: {{ item.metadata.name }} - Status: {{ item.status.phase }}"
      loop: "{{ unhealthy_osds }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.metadata.name }}"
      when: unhealthy_osds | length > 0

    - name: Report when all OSD pods are healthy
      debug:
        msg: "All {{ ceph_osd_pods.resources | length }} Ceph OSD pods are Running."
      when: unhealthy_osds | length == 0

    - name: Generate troubleshooting report
      template:
        src: templates/troubleshooting-report.j2
        dest: "/tmp/odf-troubleshooting-report-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.txt"

    - name: Show report location
      debug:
        msg: "Report written to /tmp/odf-troubleshooting-report-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.txt"
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Create the Report Template
cat > templates/troubleshooting-report.j2 << 'EOF'
ODF Storage Troubleshooting Report
Generated: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
=====================================

Summary:
- Pending PVCs: {{ failed_pvcs | length }}
- Unhealthy OSD Pods: {{ unhealthy_osds | length }}

{% if failed_pvcs | length > 0 %}
Pending PVCs:
{% for pvc in failed_pvcs %}
- Name: {{ pvc.metadata.name }}
  Namespace: {{ pvc.metadata.namespace }}
  Storage Class: {{ pvc.spec.storageClassName | default('N/A') }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if unhealthy_osds | length > 0 %}
Unhealthy OSD Pods:
{% for osd in unhealthy_osds %}
- Name: {{ osd.metadata.name }}
  Status: {{ osd.status.phase }}
  Node: {{ osd.spec.nodeName | default('N/A') }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

Recommendations:
{% if failed_pvcs | length > 0 %}
- Check storage class availability and configuration
- Verify sufficient storage capacity with `oc describe pvc <name> -n <namespace>`
{% endif %}
{% if unhealthy_osds | length > 0 %}
- Check node health and disk availability
- Review OSD logs with `oc logs <pod-name> -n openshift-storage`
{% endif %}
{% if failed_pvcs | length == 0 and unhealthy_osds | length == 0 %}
- No action required; storage subsystem appears healthy
{% endif %}
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Run the Playbook and Inspect the Report
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/storage-troubleshooting.yml
cat /tmp/odf-troubleshooting-report-*.txt
Deliverable: A file named /tmp/odf-troubleshooting-report-<epoch>.txt exists and contains a "Summary" section with numeric counts for pending PVCs and unhealthy OSD pods, matching the counts shown in the playbook's debug output during the run. On a healthy cluster with no test workloads, both counts should be 0 and the recommendations section should state no action is required.

Task 3: Verify ODF System State Against Validation Rules
Subtask 3.1: Create a State Verification Playbook Using Facts
This playbook gathers ODF-related facts with set_fact and validates them against fixed rules (minimum storage nodes, expected storage classes present).

cat > playbooks/verify-odf-state.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Verify ODF System State
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    odf_namespace: "openshift-storage"
    validation_rules:
      min_storage_nodes: 3

  tasks:
    - name: Gather storage class facts
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: storage.k8s.io/v1
        kind: StorageClass
      register: all_storage_classes

    - name: Set ODF storage class fact
      set_fact:
        odf_storage_classes: "{{ all_storage_classes.resources | selectattr('provisioner', 'search', 'ceph') | list }}"

    - name: Gather Ceph cluster facts
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: ceph.rook.io/v1
        kind: CephCluster
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: ceph_clusters

    - name: Set Ceph cluster health fact
      set_fact:
        ceph_cluster_healthy: "{{ ceph_clusters.resources | selectattr('status.phase', 'equalto', 'Ready') | list | length > 0 }}"

    - name: Gather node facts
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: Node
      register: cluster_nodes

    - name: Set storage node fact
      set_fact:
        storage_nodes: "{{ cluster_nodes.resources | selectattr('metadata.labels', 'defined') | selectattr('metadata.labels', 'contains', 'cluster.ocs.openshift.io/openshift-storage') | list }}"

    - name: Validate minimum storage node count
      set_fact:
        storage_nodes_valid: "{{ (storage_nodes | length) >= validation_rules.min_storage_nodes }}"

    - name: Display system state summary
      debug:
        msg:
          - "ODF storage classes found: {{ odf_storage_classes | length }}"
          - "Ceph cluster healthy: {{ ceph_cluster_healthy }}"
          - "Storage nodes found: {{ storage_nodes | length }} (minimum required: {{ validation_rules.min_storage_nodes }})"
          - "Storage node requirement met: {{ storage_nodes_valid }}"

    - name: Fail the play if validation rules are not met
      fail:
        msg: "ODF validation failed: insufficient storage nodes or unhealthy Ceph cluster."
      when: not storage_nodes_valid or not ceph_cluster_healthy
      ignore_errors: yes
EOF
Note: selectattr('metadata.labels', 'contains', ...) treats the labels dictionary as a collection of its keys, which is valid in Jinja2/Ansible for checking label presence.

Subtask 3.2: Run the Validation Playbook
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/verify-odf-state.yml
Deliverable: The debug task prints a four-line summary showing counts for storage classes and storage nodes, the Ceph health boolean, and whether the node requirement was met. If validation rules are not satisfied (for example, fewer than three labeled storage nodes in a compact or single-node test cluster), the final task reports a failure message describing exactly which rule was not met, but the play continues (ignore_errors: yes) so the full report is still visible. Record whether your cluster passed or failed each rule.

Expected Outcomes
A working set of three Ansible playbooks that check ODF namespace/storage class/Ceph cluster health, detect pending PVCs and unhealthy OSD pods, and validate cluster state against fixed rules
A generated troubleshooting report file in /tmp containing accurate counts pulled directly from live playbook data
Practical experience using kubernetes.core.k8s_info, set_fact, selectattr/rejectattr filters, and Jinja2 templates to build ODF diagnostic tooling
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Tasks fail with an authentication or "Unable to connect" error. Diagnostic hint: Run oc whoami and oc get ns directly in the same shell used to launch ansible-playbook. If those fail, your kubeconfig session has expired and the kubernetes.core modules (which rely on it by default) will fail identically.

Issue 2: ceph_clusters.resources or ceph_osd_pods.resources is always empty. Diagnostic hint: Confirm the CephCluster CRD and labels actually exist on your cluster with oc get cephcluster -n openshift-storage and oc get pods -n openshift-storage -l app=rook-ceph-osd. A minimal test cluster without full ODF deployed will legitimately return zero resources; this is not a playbook bug.

Conclusion
In this lab, students built a small but complete Ansible automation suite for troubleshooting OpenShift Data Foundation storage, using the kubernetes.core collection to query namespace, storage class, PVC, pod, and CephCluster state directly from a live OpenShift cluster. Students practiced turning raw Kubernetes API data into actionable diagnostics by using set_fact, selectattr, and rejectattr to isolate pending PVCs and unhealthy OSD pods, and by rendering that data into a readable Jinja2 report. The final validation playbook demonstrated how fixed operational rules, such as a minimum storage node count, can be checked automatically and reported clearly. These same patterns, gathering facts, filtering for problem states, and generating structured reports, apply directly to real ODF production environments, and students should next explore extending these playbooks with scheduled execution (via cron or an Ansible AWX/Tower job template) and integrating alerts with existing monitoring pipelines such as Prometheus Alertmanager
